# Digital Scoundrel

You are an AI pair-programmer working on a faithful digital adaptation of the solo card game "Scoundrel".
Primary goal: implement the core game loop with minimal UI (text + simple card visuals).
Secondary goal: polish UI, assets, accessibility & interactions.

## 0 Non-Negotiables

- Faithful rules implementation comes before polish.
- Prefer deterministic, testable game logic.
- Separate:
  - Pure game state transitions (engine)
  - Randomness (deck shuffle / draw)
  - UI rendering & input
- Every rule should map to:
  - a state field
  - a transition function
  - a validation function
  - tests

## 1 Game Overview (Core Loop)

### 1.1 Premise

The player explores a dungeon represented by a standard deck of cards. The player’s goal is to survive the entire deck by managing health, equipment, and room-by-room decisions.

### 1.2 Core Interaction

At any point the player is in exactly one of:

- Starting a run (initialize, shuffle, deal)
- In a room (4 face-up cards available to resolve one-by-one)
- Between rooms (cleanup / draw next room)
- Run ended (win/lose)

### 1.3 Win / Lose

- Win: player survives until the deck is exhausted AND the final room is resolved.
- Lose: player HP reaches 0 (or below) at any time.

Acceptance:

- End conditions are deterministic and unit-tested.

## 2 Components & Terminology

### 2.1 Scoundrel Deck

44 cards: Standard 52-card deck with the following removed:
- Red Aces: A♥, A♦ (2 cards)
- Red face cards: J♥, Q♥, K♥, J♦, Q♦, K♦ (6 cards)

This leaves:
- Monsters (♠/♣): A-K = 26 cards (includes black Aces and face cards)
- Potions (♥): 2-10 = 9 cards  
- Weapons (♦): 2-10 = 9 cards

### 2.2 Card Types (by suit)

- Spades: Monsters
- Clubs: Monsters
- Hearts: Healing (food/potions)
- Diamonds: Weapons

### 2.3 Rank Values

Define `rankValue`:

- A = 1
- 2–10 = pip value
- J = 11
- Q = 12
- K = 13

Acceptance:

- One canonical mapping used everywhere.
- Tests confirm all 13 ranks map correctly.

### 2.4 “Room”

A room is a set of 4 face-up cards drawn from the deck (or fewer when deck runs low). The player resolves cards in that room until none remain, then proceeds.

## 3 Player State

### 3.1 Health

- `hp`: integer
- `hpMax`: integer
Start values (default, unless overridden in config):
- `hpMax = 20`
- `hp = hpMax`

Healing:

- Healing cannot increase HP above `hpMax`.

Acceptance:

- Healing clamps.
- Damage reduces hp.
- Game ends on hp <= 0.

### 3.2 Weapon (Equipment)

Player may hold at most one weapon:

- `weapon`: null | { value: int } where value is the weapon’s rankValue
When a new weapon is taken:
- replaces current weapon (old weapon discarded).

Acceptance:

- Exactly one weapon at a time.
- Replacing weapon discards old.

### 3.3 “Monster Memory” (Same-Suit Rule)

Track whether player is “in combat sequence” with a particular monster suit.
Rule intent: when using a weapon, monsters of the same suit must be fought in non-decreasing order (you cannot fight a smaller one after fighting a bigger one of that suit with your weapon).

Implementation:

- Track `lastWeaponHitBySuit: { spades: int|null, clubs: int|null }`
- Only applies when using weapon damage (not when taking full damage without weapon).

Acceptance:

- If lastWeaponHitBySuit[suit] = X, then you may only weapon-attack a monster of that suit with value >= X.
- If player chooses to take full damage (no weapon used), this constraint does not update.

## 4 Setup / Initialization

### 4.1 Start Run

- Create Scoundrel deck (44 cards - see section 2.1)
- Shuffle (seedable RNG)
- Initialize player (hp, hpMax, weapon=null, lastWeaponHitBySuit reset)
- Draw first room (up to 4 cards)

Acceptance:

- Given a fixed RNG seed, the entire run is reproducible.
- Initial room is correct size based on remaining deck.

## 5 Turn Structure (Room Resolution)

In a room, there are N face-up cards (N <= 4).
Player repeatedly selects one available card and resolves it.
Resolved cards are discarded.

### 5.1 Player Action Options per Card

Depending on card type:

#### A Monster (spades/clubs)

Player chooses one of:

1) Fight with weapon (if weapon exists AND same-suit rule allows)
2) Fight without weapon (take full monster value as damage)

Effects:

- If fighting with weapon:
  - Damage = max(0, monsterValue - weaponValue)
  - Update lastWeaponHitBySuit[monsterSuit] = monsterValue
- If fighting without weapon:
  - Damage = monsterValue
  - Do NOT update lastWeaponHitBySuit

Monster card always removed from room after resolution.

Acceptance:

- Weapon fight is blocked by same-suit constraint.
- Damage calculation is correct and clamped at 0.

#### B Healing (hearts)

Player resolves:

- hp = min(hpMax, hp + healValue)
Card removed from room.

Acceptance:

- Healing works with clamp.
- No other side effects.

#### C Weapon (diamonds)

Player resolves:

- Equip weapon with weaponValue (discard old if present)
Card removed from room.

Acceptance:

- Equipping replaces.
- lastWeaponHitBySuit not affected.

## 6 Special Rule: Flee / “Run Away”

Once per room, the player may choose to flee:

- Select exactly one card in the current room to “flee from”
- That card is placed at the bottom of the deck (preserving its identity)
- The remaining cards in the room are discarded (or resolved as per rules baseline)

IMPORTANT: Implement as configurable because variants exist.
Use default baseline:

- Flee ends the room immediately.
- The chosen card goes to bottom of deck.
- All other room cards are discarded (not resolved).

Config:

- `fleeEnabled: true`
- `fleePerRoomLimit: 1`

State:

- `room.fleeUsed: boolean`

Acceptance:

- Flee cannot be used more than once per room.
- The bottom-of-deck insertion is correct.
- Remaining room cards leave the room and do not affect hp/weapon.

(If user later provides the exact PDF rule wording that differs, update this section and adjust tests accordingly.)

## 7) Room Transition

When room is empty (or flee ends room):

- If deck has cards:
  - Draw next room (up to 4)
  - Reset room.fleeUsed = false
- If deck empty:
  - Player wins (if hp > 0)

Acceptance:

- Correct handling when deck has 1–3 cards remaining.
- Victory triggers only after final room is completed/ended.

## 8 Data Model (Implementation Guidance)

### 8.1 Canonical Types

- Card:
  - id: unique string (stable)
  - suit: "spades"|"clubs"|"hearts"|"diamonds"
  - rank: "A"|"2"|...|"10"|"J"|"Q"|"K"
  - value: int (derived)
  - type: "monster"|"heal"|"weapon" (derived)

- GameState:
  - rngSeed: number|string
  - deck: Card[] (top at end or start; choose one convention and document)
  - discard: Card[]
  - room:
    - cards: Card[]
    - fleeUsed: boolean
  - player:
    - hp: int
    - hpMax: int
    - weapon: null | { value: int }
    - lastWeaponHitBySuit: { spades: int|null, clubs: int|null }
  - status: "init"|"in_room"|"won"|"lost"

### 8.2 Transition Functions (Pure)

Implement pure reducers like:

- startRun(seed, config) -> GameState
- drawRoom(state) -> GameState
- resolveCard(state, cardId, choice) -> GameState
- fleeRoom(state, cardId) -> GameState

Acceptance:

- No mutation of input state (copy-on-write).
- All transitions validate preconditions and return either:
  - newState
  - or an error object (preferred for UI messaging)

## 9 Minimal UI Requirements (v1)

- Display:
  - HP (current/max)
  - Equipped weapon value (or none)
  - Room cards (face-up): show suit + rank + derived type + value
  - Whether flee used this room
  - Remaining deck count
  - Discard count

- Inputs:
  - Click/select a card
  - If monster: choose “weapon fight” or “no-weapon”
  - If heal/weapon: single resolve action
  - Flee action: pick a card to flee from (if available)

Acceptance:

- Player can complete a full run via UI without console commands.
- Invalid actions are blocked with a clear message.

## 10 Config / Variants (Do Not Overbuild)

Provide a single config object:

- hpMax (default 20)
- roomSize (default 4)
- fleeEnabled (default true)
- fleePerRoomLimit (default 1)
- rulesetName (string)

Acceptance:

- Defaults implement the base game.
- Config is injected into engine, not hard-coded in UI.

## 11 Testing Requirements

### 11.1 Unit Tests (Engine)

Must cover:

- Deck creation (52 unique cards)
- Rank mapping
- Room drawing counts (4 / <4)
- Heal clamp
- Weapon replace
- Monster damage formula (weapon and no-weapon)
- Same-suit non-decreasing constraint
- Flee: bottom-of-deck insertion + room discard behavior
- Win/lose conditions

### 11.2 Determinism Test

Given fixed seed:

- first room composition is identical across runs
- full run sequence is reproducible with same player choices

Acceptance:

- Tests run headless; no dependency on Love2D graphics.

## 12 Technical Notes (Love2D)

- Use a single source of truth: `GameState`.
- Love2D layer:
  - collects input
  - calls pure engine transitions
  - renders state
- Randomness:
  - Seed RNG at startRun
  - Do not call RNG anywhere else except shuffle/draw utilities.

Acceptance:

- Engine can be executed in isolation (Lua module) without Love2D runtime.

## 13 Explicit Non-Goals (v1)

Do NOT implement yet:

- Meta progression / upgrades / floors
- New card types / modified deck
- Animations, particles, sound
- Save/load, unlocks
- Multiple characters or classes

Acceptance:

- Any PR adding v2 features must be rejected unless explicitly requested.

## 14 Open Items / Known Ambiguities

- Flee rule varies by ruleset. Current implementation uses the baseline described in section 6.
- If a provided PDF ruleset contradicts any section, update the rule text FIRST, then update tests, then update engine.

Acceptance:

- Rules are centralized here; code follows this doc.
